<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="chapter-gt3-synthetic-geometry">
  <title>GT.3 — Synthetic Geometry</title>
  <introduction>
  <xi:include href="./jc-geometry-gt3-outcome.ptx"/>
<p>This chapter develops the definitions, axioms, theorems and proof skills required for GT.3. The blue block above is the official learning outcome; the purple lists in each section are our practical learning intentions.</p>
    <p>Synthetic geometry studies points, lines, angles and figures through definitions, diagrams and logical argument. We begin with familiar constructions and observations, then learn how an axiom or a theorem justifies a conclusion. Drawing accurately can help us discover a result; a proof explains why it must hold.</p>
    <p>The official outcome above is reproduced directly from the specification. The teaching explanations and examples that follow are separate from that wording.</p>
    <p>The full Irish geometry reference is <xref ref="appendix-b-geometry"/>. It gives a numbered index and a direct link to the official NCCA document, which remains authoritative for statements and required proofs.</p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="gt3-language-and-notation">
    <title>Points, lines, rays and angles</title>
    <objectives>
      <title>Learning Intentions</title>
      <introduction><p>After studying this section, you should be able to:</p></introduction>
      <ol>
        <li><p>Distinguish a line, segment and ray, and use geometrical notation carefully.</p></li>
        <li><p>Identify the vertex and arms of an angle and interpret a labelled diagram.</p></li>
        <li><p>Explain the difference between a measured observation and a deductive conclusion.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </objectives>
    <p><em>Linked learning outcome:</em> <xref ref="jc-outcomes-geometry-gt3" text="title"/>.</p>
    <p>Points such as <m>A</m>, <m>B</m> and <m>C</m> indicate positions. The line <m>AB</m> continues without end in both directions. The segment <m>[AB]</m> has endpoints <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>. The ray <m>[AB</m> begins at <m>A</m> and passes through <m>B</m>.</p>
    <p>The length <m>|AB|</m> is a number. The angle <m>\angle BAC</m> has vertex <m>A</m>, because <m>A</m> is the middle letter. The measure of that angle can be written <m>|\angle BAC|</m>. We often speak simply of an angle being equal to another, when we mean equal in measure.</p>
    <p><term>Collinear</term> points lie on the same line. <term>Perpendicular</term> lines meet at a right angle. <term>Parallel</term> lines lie in the same plane and do not meet; in the formal geometry of Appendix B coincident lines are also counted as parallel.</p>
    <exercise xml:id="gt3-notation-check">
      <statement><p>In <m>\triangle ABC</m>, write the angle at <m>C</m> with three letters. Name the side opposite <m>C</m> and write its length.</p></statement>
      <answer><p>The angle is <m>\angle ACB</m>, the opposite side is <m>[AB]</m>, and its length is <m>|AB|</m>.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
  </section>


  <section xml:id="gt3-undefined-terms">
    <title>The nine undefined terms</title>
    <objectives><title>Learning Intentions</title><ul>
      <li><p>Identify the nine undefined terms in Appendix B.</p></li>
      <li><p>Explain the difference between an undefined term and a defined term.</p></li>
    </ul></objectives>
    <p><em>Undefined</em> does not mean mysterious. Every mathematical system needs a few starting ideas, which we describe informally rather than trying to define using other words.</p>
    <p><alert>Appendix B gives exactly nine undefined terms:</alert></p>
    <ul>
      <li><p><term>Point</term> — a position without size.</p></li>
      <li><p><term>Line</term> — straight and extending indefinitely in both directions.</p></li>
      <li><p><term>Plane</term> — the flat setting for our geometry.</p></li>
      <li><p><term>Ray</term> — a half-line starting at one point.</p></li>
      <li><p><term>Angle</term> — an idea represented by a vertex and two arms.</p></li>
      <li><p><term>Degree</term> — a measure of angle.</p></li>
      <li><p><term>Length</term> — the measure of the extent of a segment.</p></li>
      <li><p><term>Real number</term> — a number on the real number line.</p></li>
      <li><p><term>Set</term> — a collection of objects.</p></li>
    </ul>
    <p>Those short descriptions are <em>not</em> formal definitions. For example, a <term>segment</term> and a <term>midpoint</term> are <em>defined</em> using these more basic ideas.</p>
    <exercise xml:id="gt3-undefined-check">
      <statement><p>Is <em>midpoint</em> an undefined term? Explain using the ideas needed to define it.</p></statement>
      <answer><p>No. A midpoint is a point on a segment whose distances to its endpoints are equal.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="gt3-defined-terms-1">
    <title>Definitions 1–18 — Lines, distance and angles</title>
    <objectives><title>Learning Intentions</title><ul>
      <li><p>Read the notation for lines, segments, rays and lengths.</p></li>
      <li><p>Classify angles and use the language of bisectors and perpendicularity.</p></li>
    </ul></objectives>
    <p>These brief explanations follow the numbered definitions in Appendix B. Learn each with a labelled diagram rather than as an isolated list.</p>
    <p><alert>Definitions 1–5: points and distances</alert></p>
    <ul>
      <li><p><alert>1.</alert> A segment <m>[AB]</m> joins two endpoints; a ray <m>[AB</m> starts at <m>A</m> and passes through <m>B</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>2.</alert> Points are <term>collinear</term> when they lie on one straight line.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>3.</alert> A triangle is formed by three non-collinear points and their joining segments.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>4.</alert> <m>|AB|</m> is the distance between <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, and the length of <m>[AB]</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>5.</alert> The midpoint <m>M</m> of <m>[AB]</m> satisfies <m>|AM|=|MB|</m>.</p></li>
    </ul>
    <p><alert>Definitions 6–18: angles</alert></p>
    <ul>
      <li><p><alert>6.</alert> A set is <term>convex</term> if the whole segment joining any two of its points stays inside it.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>7–11.</alert> The five angle types are <term>null</term> (<m>0^\circ</m>), <term>ordinary</term> (between <m>0^\circ</m> and <m>180^\circ</m>), <term>straight</term> (<m>180^\circ</m>), <term>reflex</term> (between <m>180^\circ</m> and <m>360^\circ</m>) and <term>full</term> (<m>360^\circ</m>).</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>12.</alert> <m>\angle BAC</m> names the angle whose vertex is <m>A</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>13.</alert> An angle bisector divides an angle into two angles of equal measure.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>14.</alert> A right angle measures <m>90^\circ</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>15.</alert> An acute angle is less than <m>90^\circ</m>; an ordinary obtuse angle is greater than <m>90^\circ</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>16.</alert> Supplementary angles add to <m>180^\circ</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>17.</alert> Perpendicular lines meet at a right angle.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>18.</alert> Vertically opposite angles are the non-adjacent angles made by two intersecting lines.</p></li>
    </ul>
    <p><alert>Useful distinction:</alert> <m>[AB]</m> denotes a geometrical segment; <m>|AB|</m> denotes its numerical length.</p>
    <exercise xml:id="gt3-angle-types-check">
      <statement><p>Classify angles measuring <m>0^\circ</m>, <m>65^\circ</m>, <m>180^\circ</m>, <m>240^\circ</m>, and <m>360^\circ</m>.</p></statement>
      <answer><p>Null, ordinary (acute), straight, reflex and full, respectively.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
  </section>


  <section xml:id="gt3-defined-terms-2">
    <title>Definitions 19–46 — Shapes, similarity and circles</title>
    <objectives><title>Learning Intentions</title><ul>
      <li><p>Distinguish congruent and similar triangles.</p></li>
      <li><p>Identify and classify triangles, quadrilaterals and circles.</p></li>
      <li><p>Understand the geometric vocabulary used in proofs.</p></li>
    </ul></objectives>
    <p><alert>Definitions 19–26: triangles and parallel lines</alert></p>
    <ul>
      <li><p><alert>19.</alert> <term>Congruent triangles</term> have equal corresponding sides and angles.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>20.</alert> A <term>right-angled triangle</term> has a right angle; its opposite side is the hypotenuse.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>21.</alert> <term>Isosceles</term> means two equal sides; <term>equilateral</term> means three; <term>scalene</term> means none.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>22.</alert> <term>Parallel lines</term> are coincident or do not meet (the formal Appendix B definition).</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>23.</alert> A <term>transversal</term> is a line meeting both of two given lines.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>24.</alert> <term>Alternate angles</term> lie on opposite sides of a transversal in the specified arrangement.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>25.</alert> <term>Corresponding angles</term> occupy corresponding positions relative to two lines and their transversal.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>26.</alert> An <term>exterior angle</term> is formed by extending a triangle side; the other two interior angles are its <term>interior opposite angles</term>.</p></li>
    </ul>
    <p><alert>Definitions 27–36: polygons and similarity</alert></p>
    <ul>
      <li><p><alert>27.</alert> The <term>perpendicular bisector</term> passes through a segment's midpoint at right angles to it.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>28.</alert> A <term>polygon</term> is a closed plane figure made from straight sides that do not cross.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>29.</alert> A <term>quadrilateral</term> is a polygon with four vertices.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>30.</alert> A <term>rectangle</term> has four right angles.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>31.</alert> A <term>rhombus</term> has four equal sides.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>32.</alert> A <term>square</term> is both a rectangle and a rhombus.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>33.</alert> An <term>equilateral polygon</term> has equal sides; a <term>regular polygon</term> has equal sides and equal angles.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>34.</alert> A <term>parallelogram</term> has two pairs of parallel opposite sides.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>35.</alert> <term>Similar triangles</term> have equal corresponding angles.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>36.</alert> A point divides a segment in ratio <m>s:t</m> when the two resulting lengths have that ratio.</p></li>
    </ul>
    <p><alert>Definitions 37–41: area and circles</alert></p>
    <ul>
      <li><p><alert>37.</alert> Choosing a triangle's base determines the opposite <term>apex</term> and the perpendicular <term>height</term>.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>38.</alert> Triangle area is <m>\frac12</m> times the base length times its corresponding height.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>39.</alert> A parallelogram's height is the perpendicular distance between the lines containing its opposite sides.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>40.</alert> Circle vocabulary includes centre, radius, diameter, chord, arc, sector, semicircle, disc and circumference.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>41.</alert> A <term>cyclic quadrilateral</term> has all four vertices on a circle.</p></li>
    </ul>
    <p><alert>Definitions 42–46: tangents and special triangle points</alert></p>
    <ul>
      <li><p><alert>42.</alert> A <term>tangent</term> is a line meeting a circle at exactly one point, the <term>point of contact</term>.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>43.</alert> A <term>circumcircle</term> passes through the vertices of a triangle; its centre is the <term>circumcentre</term>.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>44.</alert> An <term>incircle</term> lies inside a triangle and touches all three sides; its centre is the <term>incentre</term>.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>45.</alert> A <term>median</term> joins a vertex to the midpoint of the opposite side; all three medians meet at the <term>centroid</term>.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>46.</alert> The <term>orthocentre</term> is where the three altitudes of a triangle meet.</p></li>
    </ul>
    <p>The final entries extend the reference beyond the basic Junior Cycle vocabulary. Course requirements must be checked against the correct level.</p>
    <p>These are concise explanations of the numbered definitions, not exact quotations. For the official wording and diagrams, consult <xref ref="appendix-b-geometry"/>.</p>
    <exercise xml:id="gt3-shape-definitions-check">
      <statement><p>Using only the definitions, explain why every square is a rectangle but not every rectangle is a square.</p></statement>
      <answer><p>A square has four right angles, so it is a rectangle. A rectangle does not have to have four equal sides, so the converse is false.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="gt3-five-axioms">
    <title>The five axioms</title>
    <objectives><title>Learning Intentions</title><ul>
      <li><p>Identify each of the five axioms by name and purpose.</p></li>
      <li><p>Know the three congruence conditions SAS, ASA and SSS.</p></li>
      <li><p>Recognise when an argument uses an axiom rather than a theorem.</p></li>
    </ul></objectives>
    <p>An <term>axiom</term> is a statement we accept as a starting point. We do not prove it using other results from our geometry. The five axioms, in the order used in Appendix B, are:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><p><alert>Axiom 1 — Two points.</alert> Exactly one straight line passes through any two distinct points.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>Axiom 2 — Ruler.</alert> Distances are non-negative and symmetric; lengths add along a segment; a specified non-negative distance can be marked uniquely along a given ray.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>Axiom 3 — Protractor.</alert> Straight angles measure <m>180^\circ</m>. An angle of a specified size determines a unique ray on each side of a line, and measures of adjacent parts add.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>Axiom 4 — Triangle congruence.</alert> <term>SAS</term> (two sides and their included angle), <term>ASA</term> (two angles and their included side), or <term>SSS</term> (three sides) establishes that two triangles are congruent when the corresponding data agree.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>Axiom 5 — Parallels.</alert> Through a given point there is exactly one line parallel to a given line.</p></li>
    </ol>
    <p>These are short student-friendly accounts, not replacements for the precise axioms. In particular, Axioms 2 and 3 contain several clauses. See <xref ref="appendix-b-axioms"/> for reference.</p>
    <exercise xml:id="gt3-axiom-test">
      <statement><p>Two triangles have two corresponding sides equal and the included angles equal. Which axiom justifies their congruence?</p></statement>
      <answer><p>Axiom 4, using the SAS congruence condition.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
  </section>


  <section xml:id="gt3-syllabus-requirements">
    <title>What do I need to know?</title>
    <objectives><title>Learning Intentions</title><ul>
      <li><p>Identify which material belongs to Junior Cycle Ordinary and Higher Level.</p></li>
      <li><p>Distinguish understanding and using a theorem from reproducing its formal proof.</p></li>
    </ul></objectives>
    <p><alert>All students:</alert> Use the appropriate geometrical vocabulary; know the five axioms; recognise and apply the relevant theorems and constructions. Work with clear diagrams and explain your reasons.</p>
    <p><alert>Junior Cycle Ordinary Level:</alert> Appendix B specifies constructions 1, 2, 4, 5, 6 and 8–15. Students study the proofs of Theorems 1–6, 9, 10, 14 and 15, and direct proofs of Corollaries 3 and 4. They study the statement and use of Theorem 13, but not its formal proof. <em>Formal proofs are not examined at Ordinary Level.</em></p>
    <p><alert>Junior Cycle Higher Level (additional content):</alert> This includes concurrent lines, constructions 3 and 7, proofs of Theorems 13 and 19, and the specified corollaries and their converses. Theorems 11 and 12 are used without requiring formal proofs. The official GT.3 outcome also identifies the statements and proofs expected at Higher Level.</p>
    <p>These summaries are based on Sections 9 and 10 of the uploaded Appendix B. Consult the <xref ref="jc-outcomes-geometry-gt3" text="title"/> for the exact learning outcome and the <url href="https://curriculumonline.ie/getmedia/ed7de7ab-2376-44f0-9c7c-b511e470c0a7/Appendix_B_English.pdf" visual="official Appendix B"/> for all level-specific detail. This is a study guide, not an additional requirement to memorise every definition word for word.</p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="gt3-axioms-theorems-proof">
    <title>Definitions, axioms, theorems and proof</title>
    <objectives>
      <title>Learning Intentions</title>
      <introduction><p>After studying this section, you should be able to:</p></introduction>
      <ol>
        <li><p>Distinguish an undefined term, a definition, an axiom, a theorem and a corollary.</p></li>
        <li><p>Explain why checking several examples is not a general proof.</p></li>
        <li><p>Justify each step of an argument using an established fact.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </objectives>
    <p><em>Linked learning outcome:</em> <xref ref="jc-outcomes-geometry-gt3" text="title"/>.</p>
    <p>A <term>definition</term> establishes what a word means. An <term>axiom</term> is accepted without proof within the chosen system. A <term>theorem</term> is established by a chain of valid arguments from axioms, definitions or earlier theorems. A <term>corollary</term> is a consequence of another result.</p>
    <p>The official geometry uses five explicitly stated axioms: two points determine a line; the ruler axiom; the protractor axiom; triangle congruence (SAS, ASA and SSS); and the axiom of parallels. Their full statements are in <xref ref="appendix-b-axioms"/>.</p>
    <p>Suppose you draw and measure fifty triangles and find that their angles add to <m>180^\circ</m>. That is evidence for a conjecture, but it is not a proof for every triangle. To prove a statement, take an arbitrary figure and justify the conclusion without relying on a particular measured diagram.</p>
    <p><em>Proof language:</em> Start with what is given, state what must be proved, add any permitted construction, and cite each relevant definition, axiom or established theorem. Never assume the result you are trying to prove.</p>
    <exercise xml:id="gt3-conjecture-or-proof">
      <statement><p>A student measures five pairs of vertically opposite angles and finds them equal. Has the student proved they are equal for every pair of intersecting lines? Explain.</p></statement>
      <answer><p>No. Measurements support the conjecture, but a general proof must apply to any pair of intersecting lines. See the next section.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="gt3-theorem-1">
    <title>Theorem 1 — Vertically opposite angles</title>
    <objectives>
      <title>Learning Intentions</title>
      <introduction><p>After studying this section, you should be able to:</p></introduction>
      <ol>
        <li><p>Identify vertically opposite and supplementary angles.</p></li>
        <li><p>State Theorem 1 and construct a proof from the straight-angle property.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </objectives>
    <p><em>Linked learning outcome:</em> <xref ref="jc-outcomes-geometry-gt3" text="title"/>.</p>
    <figure xml:id="figure-gt3-vertically-opposite">
      <caption>Two lines intersecting at <m>A</m>; the opposite angles <m>\angle BAD</m> and <m>\angle CAE</m> are marked with matching arcs. An original vector diagram following the arrangement in Appendix B, Figure 1.</caption>
      <image source="geometry/vertically-opposite-angles.svg" width="85%">
        <description>Two straight lines cross at A. B and C are opposite endpoints on one line; D and E are opposite endpoints on the other. Matching teal arcs identify the left and right vertically opposite angles.</description>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p><alert>Theorem 1.</alert> Vertically opposite angles are equal in measure.</p>
    <p><em>Proof.</em> Because <m>B,A,C</m> lie on a straight line and <m>D,A,E</m> lie on another straight line,</p>
    <me>|\angle BAD|+|\angle BAE|=180^\circ</me>
    <me>|\angle CAE|+|\angle BAE|=180^\circ.</me>
    <p>Subtract the shared angle <m>|\angle BAE|</m> from each equation. Therefore</p>
    <me>|\angle BAD|=|\angle CAE|.</me>
    <p>This proves the result without measuring either angle. The argument works for any two intersecting straight lines.</p>
    <exercise xml:id="gt3-theorem-1-exercise">
      <statement><p>Two straight lines intersect. One angle measures <m>37^\circ</m>. Find its vertically opposite angle and the two adjacent angles, giving a reason for each result.</p></statement>
      <answer><p>The vertically opposite angle is <m>37^\circ</m> by Theorem 1. Each adjacent angle measures <m>143^\circ</m>, since supplementary angles add to <m>180^\circ</m>.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="gt3-theorem-2">
    <title>Theorem 2 — Isosceles triangles</title>
    <objectives>
      <title>Learning Intentions</title>
      <introduction><p>After studying this section, you should be able to:</p></introduction>
      <ol>
        <li><p>Identify the equal sides and base angles of an isosceles triangle.</p></li>
        <li><p>Use triangle congruence to prove Theorem 2.</p></li>
        <li><p>Distinguish a theorem from its converse.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </objectives>
    <p><em>Linked learning outcome:</em> <xref ref="jc-outcomes-geometry-gt3" text="title"/>.</p>
    <figure xml:id="figure-gt3-isosceles">
      <caption>An isosceles triangle <m>ABC</m> with <m>|AB|=|AC|</m>, showing matching side ticks and base-angle arcs. Original vector diagram.</caption>
      <image source="geometry/isosceles-triangle.svg" width="75%">
        <description>Triangle ABC has A at the top and a horizontal base BC. Equal tick marks on AB and AC identify the equal sides; equal curved marks identify the angles at B and C.</description>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p><alert>Theorem 2.</alert> In an isosceles triangle the angles opposite equal sides are equal. Conversely, if two angles of a triangle are equal, then the opposite sides are equal.</p>
    <p><em>Proof of the first direction.</em> Suppose <m>|AB|=|AC|</m>. Compare <m>\triangle ABC</m> with the same triangle written in the reverse vertex order, <m>\triangle ACB</m>. We have <m>|AB|=|AC|</m>, <m>|\angle BAC|=|\angle CAB|</m>, and <m>|AC|=|AB|</m>. By SAS, the two labelled triangles are congruent. Corresponding angles at <m>B</m> and <m>C</m> are equal.</p>
    <p><em>Converse.</em> If the base angles are equal, ASA establishes the same congruence, so the opposite side lengths are equal.</p>
    <exercise xml:id="gt3-isosceles-exercise">
      <statement><p>An isosceles triangle has equal base angles, each measuring <m>68^\circ</m>. Find the apex angle and justify your calculation.</p></statement>
      <answer><p><m>180^\circ-68^\circ-68^\circ=44^\circ</m>, by Theorem 4 (the triangle angle-sum theorem).</p></answer>
    </exercise>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="gt3-theorems-3-4">
    <title>Parallel lines and the triangle angle sum</title>
    <objectives>
      <title>Learning Intentions</title>
      <introduction><p>After studying this section, you should be able to:</p></introduction>
      <ol>
        <li><p>Use the alternate-angle theorem and state its converse.</p></li>
        <li><p>Explain why the interior angles of a triangle add to <m>180^\circ</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>Use the theorem to find missing angles, giving reasons.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </objectives>
    <p><em>Linked learning outcome:</em> <xref ref="jc-outcomes-geometry-gt3" text="title"/>.</p>
    <p><alert>Theorem 3 — Alternate angles.</alert> When a transversal cuts two lines, equal alternate angles imply that the lines are parallel. Conversely, parallel lines produce equal alternate angles. A full statement includes the position of the two angles relative to the transversal.</p>
    <p><alert>Theorem 4 — Triangle angle sum.</alert> The three interior angles of every triangle add to <m>180^\circ</m>.</p>
    <figure xml:id="figure-gt3-triangle-angle-sum">
      <caption>Draw a line through <m>A</m> parallel to <m>BC</m>. The alternate angles make the three interior angles visible together along a straight line. Original vector diagram based on the configuration in Appendix B, Figure 7.</caption>
      <image source="geometry/triangle-angle-sum.svg" width="90%">
        <description>Triangle ABC with horizontal base BC. The line DAE through the apex A is parallel to BC. The three angles at A along the top line correspond to the three angles of the triangle.</description>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p><em>Proof of Theorem 4.</em> Take any triangle <m>ABC</m>. Draw a line <m>DE</m> through <m>A</m> parallel to <m>BC</m>. By the alternate-angle theorem, <m>|\angle DAB|=|\angle ABC|</m> and <m>|\angle CAE|=|\angle ACB|</m>. The three angles along the straight line <m>DE</m> sum to <m>180^\circ</m>. Hence</p>
    <me>|\angle ABC|+|\angle BAC|+|\angle ACB|=180^\circ.</me>
    <p>The same proof applies to every non-degenerate triangle. It is not restricted to the shape or measurements of the diagram.</p>
    <exercise xml:id="gt3-triangle-sum-exercise">
      <statement><p>In <m>\triangle ABC</m>, <m>|\angle A|=48^\circ</m> and <m>|\angle B|=67^\circ</m>. Find <m>|\angle C|</m> and identify the theorem used.</p></statement>
      <answer><p><m>|\angle C|=65^\circ</m>, by Theorem 4.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="gt3-further-theorems-constructions">
    <title>Further theorems and constructions</title>
    <objectives>
      <title>Learning Intentions</title>
      <introduction><p>After studying this section, you should be able to:</p></introduction>
      <ol>
        <li><p>Find a numbered theorem or construction in the geometry reference.</p></li>
        <li><p>Distinguish a theorem's use from a requirement to reproduce its formal proof.</p></li>
        <li><p>Record a valid construction clearly with the necessary arcs, lines and labels.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </objectives>
    <p><em>Linked learning outcome:</em> <xref ref="jc-outcomes-geometry-gt3" text="title"/>.</p>
    <p>Continue with the theorems on exterior angles, triangle inequalities, parallelograms, similarity, Pythagoras and circles. Use <xref ref="appendix-b-theorems"/> to find each result by its official number, and <xref ref="appendix-b-constructions"/> for the construction list.</p>
    <p>In the official Appendix B, separate sections specify what must be studied at Junior Cycle Ordinary Level, Junior Cycle Higher Level and the Leaving Certificate levels. A theorem can be needed for solving problems even when its formal proof is not examinable. Check the stated requirements rather than assuming every printed proof must be learned verbatim.</p>
    <exercise xml:id="gt3-further-proof-question">
      <statement><p>Explain the difference between knowing how to use the Pythagorean theorem and knowing how to prove it. Which theorem number is Pythagoras in Appendix B?</p></statement>
      <answer><p>Using the theorem applies the relation in a right-angled triangle. Proving it establishes why that relation always holds from accepted geometric facts. Pythagoras is Theorem 14 in Appendix B.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
  </section>
</chapter>
